 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d041: inhibitory cascade (k=6)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.2 and radius 1.52 with 6 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.42) and a 4-dim charge; 7 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 16 couplings (75% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 1.00 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 1.00 bonds per particle, largest molecule 6.5 particles, 8.0 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.104 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.101  random bonds 0.104\n  xor      0.556 vs ESN 0.529\u00b10.011  [beat]  no bonds 0.627  random bonds 0.555\n  narma10  0.309 vs ESN 0.841\u00b10.002  [below]  no bonds 0.299  random bonds 0.317\n  latch    0.908 vs ESN 0.679\u00b10.009  [beat]  no bonds 0.907  random bonds 0.915\n  mnist14  0.535 vs ESN 0.816\u00b10.007  [below]  no bonds 0.513  random bonds 0.538\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
